module

public import Rule30Open.Columns.MissedTraps
public import Rule30Open.Columns.TrapPairs

/-!
# Natural traps for 60 blocks of periods 10 to 14 whose wall traps fail the test of reach

The walls of [TenTraps](TenTraps.md), [ElevenTraps](ElevenTraps.md), [TwelveTraps](TwelveTraps.md),
[ThirteenTraps](ThirteenTraps.md) and [FourteenTraps](FourteenTraps.md), carried leftward, give traps to blocks of their
backgrounds, but entry into many of the traps carried far is false: in the game of 20 cells some node cannot reach them
([MissedTraps](MissedTraps.md)). For 60 of those blocks, 9 of period 10, 13 of period 11, 14 of period 12, 8 of period
13 and 16 of period 14, the natural scans find closed sets of windows that sampled halves driven by the block settle
into and that pass the test. For 54 blocks one trap is enough; here the kernel checks each, 46 in one declaration each
and 8 of more than 3,000 states in parts of 1,024. For six blocks the halves split between two traps apart from each
other, so entry into either alone is false, and the block is reduced to entry into one of the two, as in
[TrapPairs](TrapPairs.md).

* `natw<block>`, with `_check` (and `_<part>`, `_parts` for the large ones): the traps, each passing the check of
  [WordTraps](WordTraps.md).
* `naturalTrapsWalls`, `naturalTrapsWalls_check`, `naturalTrapsWalls_blocks`: the list, its check and its blocks.
* `naturalWalls_absorption_excludes`: for each of them, if every finitely supported right half driven by the block
  enters its trap, the center column does not eventually repeat the block.
* `natw<block>_0`, `natw<block>_1` and `natw<block>_excludes` for the blocks `0001010111`, `00101101111`,
  `000000101101`, `000010011111`, `0000001111111` and `00101101101101`: entry into one of the two traps excludes the
  block. `natw<block>_0_not_absorption` and `natw<block>_1_not_absorption`, for each pair but that of `0000001111111`,
  whose two traps of 3,032 and 718 states are too many for the kernel's check of their separation here: entry into
  either alone is false, each trap being a closed set apart from the other ([MissedTraps](MissedTraps.md)).

The tables come from `python -m computations.lock_chain --lean-wall-natural`. The natural-language argument is
`NaturalTrapsWalls.md`, beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

-- Sequential elaboration: the kernel checks would otherwise run in parallel and stack their memory.
set_option Elab.async false

namespace Rule30Open

open Ruliology.CellularAutomata

/-- **A trap that passes the check is a closed set**: its check asks every state's moves to be states, as
`WTrap.closedCheck` does, and more. -/
theorem WTrap.closedCheck_of_check (C : WTrap) (hC : C.check = true) : C.closedCheck = true := by
  obtain ⟨-, -, -, hm, hall⟩ := C.facts hC
  unfold WTrap.closedCheck
  rw [Bool.and_eq_true, decide_eq_true_eq]
  refine ⟨hm, allBelowT_of_forall (fun i hi => ?_)⟩
  have h := hall i hi
  unfold WTrap.nodeOK at h
  unfold WTrap.closedOK
  simp only [Bool.and_eq_true] at h ⊢
  exact ⟨⟨h.1.1.1, h.1.2⟩, h.2⟩

